        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you mostly play doubles, spend a lot of time at the kitchen line, and want your dinks and resets to land soft, a 16mm paddle is the safer choice. If you like to drive third shots, win hand battles and finish points with speed, a 14mm paddle gives you more pop for the same swing. Most recreational players who are unsure end up happier with 16mm, because control mistakes cost more points at that level than a lack of power does.
          </p>

          <p>
            That rule of thumb holds because of how a honeycomb core behaves. A thicker core flexes less across the face and absorbs more of the ball's energy at impact, so the ball comes off slightly slower and more predictably. A thinner core is stiffer in a different way: there is less material to soak up the hit, so more energy goes back into the ball and the paddle feels livelier and louder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference is real but smaller than marketing suggests. Face material, surface texture, paddle shape, weight and whether the paddle is thermoformed all change how a paddle plays, often by more than two millimetres of core. A thick paddle with a stiff carbon face and heavy swing weight can hit harder than a thin paddle with a soft fiberglass face. Thickness is one input, not the whole story.
          </p>

          <p>
            So treat thickness as a tie-breaker between paddles you already like on shape and weight, or as a corrective. If your drives sail long, go thicker. If your blocks and counters feel dead and you cannot put balls away, go thinner. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">tested pickleball paddle picks</a> list core thickness for every model so you can compare like with like.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Thick for touch, thin for pop.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 16mm core absorbs more of the ball's energy and is more forgiving. A 14mm or 13mm core returns more energy and plays faster, but asks more of your control. Pick the one that fixes your most common miss.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-thickness-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Core Thickness Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every modern paddle has a polypropylene honeycomb core sandwiched between two face sheets, usually carbon fiber, fiberglass or a hybrid. The core is a grid of small cells, a bit like a beehive, and its thickness is simply how deep those cells are from face to face. Common thicknesses are around 13mm, 14mm, 16mm and occasionally 20mm, with some brands quoting oddities such as 15mm.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the ball hits the face, the face sheet deflects slightly and the cells behind it compress. A deeper core spreads that load through more material and damps it, which is why thick paddles feel muted and plush. A shallower core has less material behind the face, so the face rebounds more sharply and the ball leaves faster. Players describe the thin-core feel as crisp, poppy or hot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness also changes the paddle's stability. A deeper core makes the paddle more rigid against twisting on off-centre hits, so the face stays square when you catch the ball near the edge. That is one reason thick paddles feel forgiving: mishits lose less direction and pace. Thin paddles twist a little more on off-centre contact unless the maker adds weight or perimeter reinforcement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight follows thickness only loosely. A thicker core adds a few grams of polymer, but face material, edge guard and handle length matter more to the number on the scale. Two paddles of different thickness can weigh the same, and when they do, the swing weight and balance point tell you more about how they will feel than the thickness does. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">guide to choosing a paddle</a> covers weight and balance in detail.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
