        <div id="power-control" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Power vs Control: The Trade You Are Making
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power in pickleball is mostly about how much energy the paddle returns to the ball, not how hard you swing. A thinner core returns more, so the same stroke produces a slightly faster ball. That helps on drives, overheads and fast counters, where a few extra miles per hour can beat a defender's reaction. It also helps players whose swing is short or who play mostly from the kitchen line and want put-away speed without a big backswing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Control is the other side of the same coin. Because a thick core soaks up more energy, it is easier to take pace off the ball, which is exactly what you want on dinks, drops and resets. When an opponent hits hard at your feet, a 16mm paddle makes it easier to block the ball back softly into the kitchen. With a 14mm paddle, the same block is more likely to pop up and get attacked.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most doubles players, the soft game decides more points than the hard game, which is why 16mm paddles dominate recreational play. Singles players, and doubles players who prefer to drive and speed up, often move to 14mm or thinner once their touch is reliable. The thinner paddle does not give them control they lack; it rewards control they already have.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical test is to look at your misses. If your errors are mostly long drives, sailed lobs and dinks that float high, you have more power than you can manage and a thicker core will help. If your errors are mostly balls that die in the net, counters that lack pace and put-aways that get returned, a thinner core will help. Most players can tell which list is longer after a few sessions of honest attention.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK YOUR MISSES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Long and high means go thicker. Short and slow means go thinner.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Thickness is the cheapest fix for a power or control imbalance, because it changes the paddle's response without you changing your stroke.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sweet-spot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sweet Spot, Dwell Time and Feel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sweet spot is the area of the face where contact feels solid and the ball goes where you aimed. Thick paddles tend to have a larger usable sweet spot, because the deeper core keeps the face from flexing unevenly when you hit away from the centre. That makes 16mm paddles more forgiving for players whose contact point is still inconsistent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dwell time is how long the ball stays on the face during contact. A softer, thicker core lets the ball sink in a fraction more, and many players feel that as extra time to shape the shot, especially on dinks and rolls. Manufacturers often market dwell as a spin benefit. The effect on actual spin is debated and depends heavily on surface texture, but most players do report that thick paddles feel more connected on touch shots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thin paddles trade some of that dwell for a crisper, more immediate response. The ball leaves the face sooner and feels more direct. Players who like thin cores often describe the feel as more honest: you know exactly what you hit. Players who dislike them describe the same feel as harsh or twitchy, especially on hard incoming balls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spin itself comes mostly from the face material and its texture, plus your swing path. If spin is your main goal, read our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-spin/">best paddles for spin</a> and our <a href="/carbon-fiber-vs-fiberglass-pickleball-paddle/">carbon fiber vs fiberglass comparison</a> before you worry about thickness. A raw carbon face on a 16mm core will usually generate more spin than a smooth fiberglass face on a 14mm core.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vibration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Vibration, Noise and Your Arm
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thicker cores damp vibration better. When the ball hits a 16mm paddle, more of the shock is absorbed in the honeycomb before it reaches the handle, so less of it travels up your forearm. Players dealing with tennis elbow or wrist pain are commonly advised to choose a thicker, heavier paddle for exactly this reason, and our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle-for-tennis-elbow/">best paddles for tennis elbow</a> lean heavily toward 16mm cores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thin paddles transmit more of the hit to your hand. On clean contact that feels lively; on mishits and hard blocks it can sting. If you play several hours a day, or you already have a sore elbow, the cumulative vibration of a thin, light paddle is worth taking seriously. Adding a little weight with lead tape and a cushioned overgrip can help, but thickness is the bigger lever.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise follows the same pattern. Thin paddles are louder, with a sharper pop at contact, while thick paddles produce a duller sound. That matters more than it sounds: some communities, homeowner associations and indoor facilities have rules or preferences about paddle noise, and a few specifically list quieter paddles. If you play somewhere with noise complaints, a thick core is the easy win.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this is medical advice. If you have persistent elbow, wrist or shoulder pain, see a doctor or physical therapist. Equipment changes can reduce the load, but technique, grip pressure and rest usually matter more than the paddle.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ranges, Not Test Results</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">13–20mm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common core thicknesses</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">7.3–8.5 oz</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical paddle weight range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">16mm</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most common doubles choice</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Max length plus width per USA Pickleball</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="other-thicknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 13mm, 20mm and Everything Between
          </h2>

          <p>
            13mm paddles sit at the fast end of the range. They suit advanced players who drive a lot, play singles, or want maximum put-away speed from a compact swing. For newer players they are usually a mistake, because the extra pop exaggerates every control error. If you are considering 13mm, you should already be comfortable resetting hard balls with a 14mm or 16mm paddle.
          </p>

          <p>
            20mm paddles were once a niche control option and are now uncommon in mainstream lineups. They are very stable and very soft, which suits players who live at the kitchen line and care little about drives. The trade-off is a noticeable loss of pace on anything but a full swing. If you find one you like, it can be a good choice for players with arm pain or those who play mainly social doubles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some brands quote in-between figures such as 15mm, or offer the same paddle in two thicknesses. Those pairs are the cleanest way to feel the difference, because shape, face and handle stay the same. If a shop or demo programme lets you hit both versions of one paddle back to back, that is the single most useful test you can do before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermoformed paddles complicate the picture. Their sealed, foam-injected edges and unibody construction add power and stability regardless of core thickness, so a thermoformed 16mm paddle can play faster than a traditional 14mm one. Read our <a href="/thermoformed-pickleball-paddle/">thermoformed paddle guide</a> if you are comparing across construction types, and check our <a href="/usa-pickleball-approved-paddles/">USA Pickleball approved paddles guide</a> if you plan to play sanctioned events.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing the Right Thickness for Your Game
          </h2>

          <p>
            Newer players should start with 16mm. The bigger sweet spot, softer response and lower vibration make it easier to learn the soft game, which is where recreational doubles is won. You can always move thinner later once your touch is reliable; moving thicker after building habits on a hot paddle is harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Intermediate doubles players who feel their drives and counters lack bite are good candidates for 14mm. Keep everything else similar, ideally the same paddle in a thinner core, so you can tell whether the change helped. Give it a few sessions; the first week on a livelier paddle usually produces a few extra long balls while your hands adjust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Singles players and aggressive drivers often prefer 14mm or 13mm because they hit more full swings and need the pace. Players with arm pain, players who mainly dink, and players who play long sessions often prefer 16mm or thicker. Weight and balance should be chosen alongside thickness, not after it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not expect thickness to fix technique. A thinner paddle will not give you a drive you do not have, and a thicker paddle will not save dinks hit with a loose wrist. Use thickness to fine-tune a paddle that already suits you, then spend the rest of your effort on practice. When you are ready to buy, our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddle rankings</a> sort picks by core thickness and play style.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Copying a pro's thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Professional players often use thin, hot paddles because their control is already excellent. Copying their setup before your touch is reliable usually adds unforced errors. Choose for the misses you actually make, not for the shots you see on television, and move thinner only when your soft game holds up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging thickness in isolation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Face material, texture, shape, weight and thermoforming all change power and feel. A thick carbon paddle can outhit a thin fiberglass one. Compare paddles that differ mainly in thickness, ideally the same model in two cores, before concluding that thickness is what you liked or disliked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring your arm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin, light paddles transmit more vibration. If you already have elbow or wrist discomfort, or play many hours a week, a thin core can make it worse. Choose a thicker, slightly heavier paddle and see a professional if pain persists, rather than playing through it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching right before a tournament</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving between 16mm and 14mm changes your timing on dinks, blocks and drives. Switching days before an event usually costs more points than the new paddle gains. Make the change early in a season and give yourself several sessions to recalibrate your touch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming thicker means heavier</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Core thickness adds only a few grams. Edge guard, face material and handle length matter more to total weight and swing weight. Check the listed weight and balance separately, and add lead tape if you want more mass, rather than buying a thicker core to get it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 14mm or 16mm pickleball paddle better for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most beginners, 16mm is the better choice. The thicker core is more forgiving on off-centre hits, takes pace off the ball more easily, and transmits less vibration. Those traits make it easier to learn dinks, drops and resets, which decide most recreational doubles points. Move to 14mm later if you want more pop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a 16mm paddle have less power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, for the same swing. A thicker core absorbs more of the ball's energy, so the ball leaves slightly slower. The difference is modest, and face material, weight and thermoformed construction can outweigh it. Many players find a 16mm paddle has plenty of power once they use a full swing on drives.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do thicker paddles give more spin?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. Spin comes mostly from the face texture, face material and your swing path. Thicker cores may increase dwell time slightly, which some players feel helps them shape shots, but a gritty carbon face matters far more. Choose the face first if spin is your priority, then pick thickness for feel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are 13mm paddles allowed in tournaments?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                USA Pickleball does not set a minimum core thickness. What matters for sanctioned play is that the specific paddle model is on the approved list and meets the organisation's size, surface and performance standards. Check the current approved list for the exact model rather than assuming any thickness is or is not allowed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 16mm better for tennis elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It usually helps. Thicker cores damp more vibration before it reaches the handle, and slightly heavier paddles reduce the shock of fast incoming balls. Combine a 16mm core with a comfortable grip size and relaxed grip pressure. Persistent pain still needs a doctor or physical therapist, not just a new paddle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I feel the difference between 14mm and 16mm?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most players can, especially on blocks, dinks and hard counters. The 14mm version feels livelier and louder; the 16mm feels softer and more stable. The clearest way to judge is to hit both thicknesses of the same model back to back, since that removes differences in shape, face and handle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should singles players use a thinner paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many do. Singles involves more full swings, passing shots and drives, so the extra pop from a 14mm or 13mm core is useful. Singles players who rely on touch and placement, or who have arm discomfort, can still do well with 16mm. Choose based on your misses rather than the format alone.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

